---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ArticleSchema from "../../components/ArticleSchema.astro";
import FaqSection from "../../components/FaqSection.astro";
import { site, description } from "../../content";
const path = "/guides/discount-discipline";
const title = "Give every Shopify email offer a margin owner";
const desc = "Design an offer policy that survives overlapping journeys, checkout rules and refunds.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Must the first cart message contain no discount?",
    "answer": "That is a candidate strategy, not a universal rule. Honor existing promises and test whether information or an incentive solves the actual barrier."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is free shipping always better than a percentage offer?",
    "answer": "No. Compare actual subsidy, product margin, order mix and customer response; the hypothetical example is not a recommendation for every store."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does preventing email overlap prevent code stacking?",
    "answer": "No. Message coordination and Shopify discount combination settings need separate checks."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should finance approve?",
    "answer": "The cost model, contribution floor, eligible products and exception process, using actual store costs and refund behavior."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/guides">← All operator guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Give every Shopify email offer a margin owner</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Design an offer policy that survives overlapping journeys, checkout rules and refunds.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Start with the economic job</h2><p>Decide whether the offer is intended to acquire a first order, clear a particular SKU, support replenishment or recover a stalled purchase. A blanket ban on discounts and a code in every email both avoid the actual decision. Separate promised signup benefits from discretionary recovery incentives: do not withhold an advertised welcome offer merely because the customer enters a cart journey.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Build the offer register</h2><p>Record owner, eligible audience, included products, excluded products, minimum spend, benefit, expiry, reuse policy, combination settings and approval date. Finance supplies the contribution floor using actual product and fulfillment costs; retention supplies the audience and customer promise. Record who may approve an exception and when it expires. Exclude gift cards, deposits or restricted items only when the actual terms require it, not because a generic template assumes they all behave alike.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Hypothetical margin example</h2><p>Suppose an order has $100 in merchandise before tax, $55 product cost and $10 variable fulfillment/payment cost, with no other costs included. A $10 discount leaves $90 revenue and $25 contribution: 100 − 10 − 55 − 10 = 25. If the proposed alternative costs the merchant $6 in shipping subsidy, contribution is $29 under those same assumptions. These are hypothetical inputs, not merchant results or a free-shipping recommendation. Returns, shipping revenue, variable fees and product mix can change the comparison.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Turn policy into an audience decision</h2><p>For cart recovery, test whether an informative reminder resolves shipping or product uncertainty before adding an incentive. For a limited release, confirm inventory and the advertised deadline; do not manufacture scarcity. For replenishment, check existing subscription benefits before offering another code. For loyal buyers, test relevant access or service against a monetary offer without assuming all repeat buyers are insensitive to price. Use observed behavior carefully; prior redemption alone does not prove someone would refuse full price.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Checkout combinations are a separate gate</h2><p>Shopify documents that each discount must allow a proposed combination for discounts to apply together. Inspect the actual product, order and shipping settings and test eligible and ineligible carts. A message scheduler preventing duplicate emails does not prevent an old valid code being redeemed during a sale. Conversely, rejecting a stack at checkout does not fix contradictory promises in two messages. Review both the communication and redemption layers.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Proposed checkout test cases</h2><p>Use a controlled checkout with a promised welcome code, a cart offer and a sitewide automatic discount. Test each alone and the intended combinations, minimum-spend boundaries, excluded variants, expiry, repeat use and shipping eligibility. Include mobile links and customer identity changes. Record the displayed explanation and final order discount allocation. These are proposed tests; no checkout outcomes are claimed here.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Coordinate the sale calendar</h2><p>Before a campaign, decide which lifecycle offers remain valid, which branches become reminder-only and how existing promises will be honored. Give support the same terms customers see. Set an explicit restoration owner and expiry for temporary changes. Link the policy to the collision matrix so that a campaign pause does not leave welcome or cart promotions running with an obsolete benefit.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Measure contribution and incrementality</h2><p>Track redemption, full-price orders, contribution after refunds, support confusion and repeat purchase for comparable cohorts. Code-attributed revenue is not proof that the discount caused the sale. A proposed randomized comparison can contrast a permitted offer with reminder-only messaging where no prior promise is broken. Decide sample needs and duration from baseline variability and the commercial risk; do not claim a universal holdout percentage or a predetermined winner.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Audit tooling without assumed defaults</h2><p>Ask Sequenzy or another candidate to demonstrate eligibility, code creation, expiry, reuse and suppression using your policy. Verify where each rule executes and what happens if code creation or event delivery fails. Keep a rollback path to approved reminder content instead of inventing an emergency offer. No native discount ladder, predicted sensitivity feature or pricing model is assumed here.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Primary references and related decisions</h2><p>Primary guidance checked 24 November 2026; scope is limited to the points attributed above. Store configuration and jurisdiction-specific review remain necessary.</p><ul><li><a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/discounts/discount-combinations">Shopify discount combinations</a></li></ul><p><a class="link" href="/guides/consent-compliance">Consent compliance</a> · <a class="link" href="/guides/deliverability">Deliverability</a> · <a class="link" href="/guides/flow-collision-prevention">Flow collision prevention</a></p></div></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
